  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Quiet and pleasant weather today, rain chances return \nlate Wednesday lasting into the weekend. \n\nAfter a chilly start this morning with some areas in the low \n40s, temperatures are rebounding nicely with seasonable highs \nexpected this afternoon, in the upper 70s to low 80s. High \npressure remains in place across the Mid-Atlantic this \nafternoon, keeping skies clear and conditions dry. The humidity\nremains relatively low due to a general northerly flow. This is\nshort-lived, as by late Wednesday, the high shifts offshore \ninto the Atlantic, with southerly flow returning to the region. \nThis will cause an increase in moisture, with dew points rising\nback into the upper 60s to around 70 degrees. A subtle \nshortwave trough will also traverse across the area Wednesday \nafternoon, triggering isolated showers/storms to form, mainly \nalong/east of the Blue Ridge into Wednesday evening.\n\nA cold front then approaches the area Thursday afternoon, \nincreasing storm chances through Saturday as the front stalls \nalong the East Coast. Although no washout days are expected,\nscattered to widespread showers/storms will develop Thursday \nand Friday afternoons. Storms that move over the same areas \nrepeatedly could lead to localized flash flooding both days, \nespecially in the Piedmont, where there is a Marginal Risk for \nexcessive rainfall.\n\nThe frontal boundary eventually pushes offshore by Sunday, with \ndrier weather expected into early next week, as high pressure builds \nback into the area once again. Early indications are that \ntemperatures will rise into the 80s/90s early next week, as high\npressure builds in both at the surface and aloft. Summer is not\nquite over just yet.",
